#   Beagle-lite index records

The record catalogue of the `.git/be/` index a lite verb reads and writes ([LITE-006]). One `abc.index("wh128", …)` family — a dog Pup stack of `(key:u64, val:u64)` rows, sorted by key, unkeyed (a key may carry many rows) — lives in the repo's OWN gitdir as `<gitdir>/be/*.lite2.idx`. Beside it, in the same dir, stands the KEYED `kv64` lane `*.kv.idx` ([BEE-024]), where a re-put OVERWRITES and a row is a mutable cell. Everything there is DERIVED: `rm -rf` it and the next run rebuilds it — and the EXTENSION IS THE FORMAT, so a file of neither live format is swept (unlinked) before either family opens ([BEE-002], [BEE-024]). Registered repos are listed in `$HOME/.config/bee/repos` ([BEE-001]), one absolute worktree path per line, written by `bee install`. The kind nibble is the LOW 4 bits of every key, so one index holds every record family and one scan sees them all.

##  Field vocabulary

The three field types every record is built out of; all are hashes, so the index never stores a name and never gives one back.

 -  `hashlet60` — the TOP 60 bits of a 20-byte sha, big-endian (`dog/WHIFF.h`
    `whiff_hashlet(s, 15)`); blob, commit and ref hashlets are ALWAYS the full 60.
 -  `path_hl` — 40 bits: the TOP 40 of the sha1 hashlet of the repo-relative
    path text, no leading slash (`hlOfText(path) >> 20`).
 -  `rev` — a 20-bit per-path arrival counter, minted oldest-first as the walk
    seals commits; `rev = 2^20-1` is the EMPTY slot spelling, never a real rev.
 -  `vnib` — the low nibble of a value, RESERVED (0) wherever the record does
    not name a field for it.
 -  `seg_hl` — the top 40/20/10 bits of ONE path segment's text hashlet
    (filename / parent dir / ancestor dir); `0` spells "absent", so a genuine
    0 hash bumps to 1 (the filename slot names nothing absent and never bumps).

##  Record kinds

Eight kinds, the nibble in `key & 0xf`. Rows are never deleted and re-puts are idempotent.

 -  `REV-BLOB` (1) — key `path_hl:40|rev:20|1`, val `blob_hl:60|vnib:4`: which
    blob this path carries at this rev.
 -  `REV-CMMT` (2) — key `path_hl:40|rev:20|2`, val `commit_hl:60|vnib:4`: the
    commit that introduced this rev of the path. A DIRECTORY path gets these and
    ONLY these ([LITE-044]): one row per commit that changed anything under it,
    which is what `lite list` attributes a dir row from.
 -  `REV-PARS` (3) — key `path_hl:40|rev:20|3`, val `par1:20|par2:20|par3:20|4`:
    this rev's parent REVS; a 4th+ parent rides a second row.
 -  `CPAR` (4) — key `commit_hl:60|4`, val `parent_hl:60|ord:4`: one row per
    parent, first parent `ord` 0; a ROOT commit gets one row with an EMPTY parent.
 -  `B2P` (5) — key `blob_hl:60|5`, val `path_hl:40|rev:20|vnib:4`: the reverse
    map, which path/rev a blob is the content of.
 -  `FSEG` (6) — key `fn_hl:40|prnt_hl:20|6`, val `seg0..seg5:10 ROOT-FIRST|
    depth:4`: the partial-path record ([LITE-011]), the last segment's hash
    plus the ancestor dir segment hashes; a pure function of the path TEXT,
    minted once at the path's rev 0, never invalidated (it names, not states).
 -  `LINK` (7) — key `fn_hl:40|par:20|7`, val `src path_hl:40|gpar:20|vnib:4`:
    the backlink SUSPECT record ([LITE-033]), one row per (dst, src) pair. Every
    slot is a truncated TEXT hashlet of the TARGET's own segments ([BEE-002]) —
    the filename's top 40 bits, the parent dir's top 20, the grandparent's top
    20, `0` spelling absent — minted from the ref TEXT alone: nothing is
    resolved, no index is read and NO repo id is recorded, so the indexing ORDER
    cannot change a key and a cross-repo target keys the same everywhere. A
    ticket code (`LITE-029`) keys as its own text with both ancestors absent, so
    a ticket's backlinks survive the thin↔fat layout move. Anchors are dropped:
    a row names files, not places.
 -  `MARK` (F) — key `ref_hl:60|F`, val `tip commit_hl:60|vnib:4`: the per-ref
    watermark, the LAST write of a run (DOG-027). `lindex` keeps its own under
    the RESERVED ref `hlOfText("lindex")`, which is no ref and so never collides.

##  What the records buy

Each query is a scan of ONE key prefix; no ODB ancestry walk happens at query time.

 -  A file's whole history is ONE prefix scan of its `path_hl`: the rows come
    back rev-ordered, each rev naming its blob, its commit and its parent revs.
 -  The commit DAG is the CPAR rows alone, which is what `lite log` walks
    ([LITE-007] `ancestry`), newest-first over a reverse Kahn.
 -  `CPAR` doubles as the INDEXED flag: a commit with any CPAR row is in the
    index, which is why a root commit still gets an empty-parent row.
 -  A commit hashlet IS an object name — `git.getHex` takes any 6..40 hexlet —
    so a row's 15-hex `hashlet60` reads date, author and subject off the ODB.
 -  `lite list`'s last-commit fuse ([LITE-017]) reads both halves off the
    entry's OWN rows, exact at any depth and walk-free: a FILE folds its
    `path_hl` chain, a DIR takes the NEWEST of its REV-CMMT rows ([LITE-044]).
    A hot dir changes in most commits, so its chain is as long as the history
    and is never read whole: revs are dense, so the newest is found by galloping
    exact-key probes, O(log) seeks for the indexer and for the fuse alike.
 -  A PARTIAL path (`abc/TCP.c`, bare `TCP.c`) resolves via one FSEG scan plus
    a pruned descent of a commit's tree (`index/resolve.js`): the tree entries
    carry the real names, so the answer is TEXT and ambiguity is a list.
    [BEE-003] FANS that over the registry — the dir of the file being read, the
    ambient repo, then every registered repo — never the REV lane, whose
    `path_hl` hashes the WHOLE path and so answers no suffix.
 -  `//name/rel` ([BEE-023]) is the spelling that SKIPS all that: the name is a
    registry basename, else the directory `$SRC_ROOT/name`, and the rest is a
    plain path under that root — one lookup, no fan-out and no ambiguity.  A
    leading `//name` alone is the CLI's context slot, the repo a run stands in,
    which is the same address without a path.
 -  A DIFF is a projection of ONE weave per path ([BEE-005], `index/dag.js` +
    `index/weave.js weaveDiff`): `repsOf` reads the rev a commit's view stands
    on (its own, else its nearest ancestor's over `CPAR` — a merge that touched
    no path of its own stands on several and they JOIN), `floorRev` finds the
    LCA inside the index over `REV-PARS` edges and DESCENDS until every folded
    rev has its parents in the fold, and the seed is the floor rev's REV-BLOB.
    So the changed-path SET still comes off the trees, while the floor, the
    seed, the layers and their edges all come off the index — the only ODB read
    left in a fold is the BLOB BYTES of a rev (a `hashlet60` IS an object name).
 -  A layer id is the rev's commit hashlet shifted one nibble, so `blame(off)`
    on an emitted token names the COMMIT that inserted it and the low nibble 0
    keeps the reserved ids (an empty side, the worktree layer) off that space.
 -  EVERY read here rides the SEEK cursor: `range`/`prefix` have been seen to
    answer NOTHING for a key a `seek` finds (index.js's own KNOWN TRAP).
    [BEE-003] FANS that over the registry — the dir of the file being read, the
    ambient repo, then every registered repo — never the REV lane, whose
    `path_hl` hashes the WHOLE path and so answers no suffix.
 -  "Who links to this?" is TWO exact-key seeks per index — `fn|0` for a
    bare-filename ref, `fn|par` for one that named the parent — over EVERY repo
    in `$HOME/.config/bee/repos` ([BEE-002]): the vals are the carriers'
    `path_hl`, named back to text by that repo's own tree descent.

##  The `kv` lane

The KEYED family, `<gitdir>/be/*.kv.idx` ([BEE-024]): `abc.index("kv64", …)` compares KEYS alone, so a re-put of one key wins at `get`, `range` and `seek` alike. That is what lets `Now: OPEN` flip to `DONE` IN PLACE, which no lite2 row can say. Its records are FILE HEADERS: the StrictMark meta pairs under a `#   Title` ([/meta/todo]) and the top-level keys of a Markdown YAML preamble. Same discipline as lite2 — derived, the kind nibble in the LOW 4 bits, one lane for every cell-like kind.

 -  `HEAD` (1) — key `path_hl:40|key_code:20|1`, val `vkind:4|payload:60`: one
    META PAIR of one file. `key_code` is the 3-char key VERBATIM at 6 bits a
    char, `ron.decode("Now")`, 18 of the 20 bits — injective, so a meta key
    never collides and a file's pairs sort stably. PATH-MAJOR: one file's whole
    block is ONE contiguous prefix scan.
 -  `YAML` (2) — the same layout with `key_code` the TOP 20 bits of the key
    text's hashlet, so `Now:` and `now:` can never meet. A nested key, a list
    and a multiline scalar are SKIPPED whole: the payload is a match token, and
    a value that will not fit one line is no token.
 -  `MARK` (F) — key `MARK_PHL|wt_code:20|F`, the reserved all-ones `path_hl`,
    so it sorts ABOVE every block; one row per WORKTREE.
 -  `path_hl` hashes the WORKTREE-QUALIFIED (absolute) path, since linked
    worktrees share the common gitdir and so share this lane ([BEE-009]); the
    block's own header row carries the owning worktree's 20-bit code.
 -  The reserved `key_code` is all-ones, the BLOCK HEADER row (kind 1): the file
    exists, and this worktree owns it.

The `vkind` nibble says what the 60-bit payload is, and only a LITERAL ranges:

 -  `LIT` (0) — the ron60 of the DESPACED, DECASED value, when it round-trips
    through the codec. It compares and RANGES exactly.
 -  `HASH` (1) — the value's hashlet60 when it does not. It only ever equals.
 -  `MARK` (2) — the max mtime observed, on the mark row.
 -  `HEAD` (E) — the owning worktree's code, on the block header row.
 -  `TOMB` (F) — the pair, or the whole file, is GONE. `kv64` has no delete, so
    a tombstone is a row like any other.

The SWEEP owns staleness, since with the value in the row there is no confirming read left to kill a stale one. It is LAZY — the first query brings the lane up, and `bee index` runs it so its summary line is honest.

 -  The candidates are every `.mkd`/`.md` the STAGE lists plus the untracked
    ones the [BEE-022] worktree walk finds under igno; a build tree never
    floods the lane and the lane never opens a source file.
 -  Every file whose mtime is AT OR PAST the mark is re-lexed and its block
    RECONCILED: put what the lex found, TOMBSTONE what vanished. A file gone
    from disk loses its whole block, this worktree's blocks only.
 -  The mark is the MAX mtime OBSERVED in the scan, one row per worktree,
    written LAST; a clock is never consulted, and an intermediate seal never
    advances it, so a crash mid-sweep costs a redundant re-lex and never marks
    an unlexed file done. Rows batch at 200, one 4 KB memtable page.
 -  Per-key normalizers are where ranges come from: `Due: 2026-08-19` becomes
    the ron60 DATE, so `Due:` takes a spelling PREFIX as a range; free text
    hashes and only equals.
 -  The sweep holds the COMPLETE path list, so a 40-bit `path_hl` collision is
    DETECTED: two live paths on one hashlet leave the index for that run and are
    read directly.
 -  `find(repo, filters)` is the one door — exact, presence or prefix per key,
    AND-intersected on `path_hl` — and a path comes back through the sweep's own
    list, never decoded from a hash. The lane decides WHICH files, never what
    to show ([TODO-003] rule): titles and bodies still come off the file.
 -  THE CONSUMER is the [BEE-025] board (`view/todo.js`), one `find` per repo per
    run: `opts.rows` hands the matched files' CELLS back beside them, so the
    open test (`Now: OPEN`), the OR'd clauses a lane intersection cannot express
    and the `Key:` absences are all answered in memory. A warm board therefore
    opens only the files it renders — `Sev:`, `Sub:` and the title come off that
    one head read, never off a packed row, and a ticket carrying no `Now:` pair
    at all is the one case that costs a read the lane did not name.

##  The suspects contract

`LINK` rows are SUSPECTS, not proof (ruling 2026-08-15): a row says a file MAY
link to the target, and precision comes from OPENING the suspect — never from
deleting a row and never from a tip-filtering hop. A removed link therefore
leaves a false suspect behind, which is exactly what keeps the never-delete,
idempotent contract of the index intact: the index only narrows the grep.

 -  ONLY TIP BLOBS are scanned, and lazily. `lindex`'s own MARK row (the
    reserved ref `hlOfText("lindex")`) holds the commit the last scan finished
    at; a run diffs mark..tip through the SAME pruning tree diff the rev
    derivation rides and tokenises only the blobs that moved. A rerun over an
    unmoved tip writes nothing at all, and the mark row is the run's LAST write.
 -  ONE BRING-UP VERB, TWO MARKS ([BEE-007]): `bee index` runs the commit walk
    and then this scan, each still no-opping off its own mark. `bringUp` — what
    the read views (`log`, `list`, `commit`) call on every invocation — stays the
    COMMIT half alone, so printing a log never lexes a blob. `bee lindex` is the
    query form and still brings the rows up bare.
 -  Link RECOGNITION is the tokenizer's and only the tokenizer's: a link is
    whatever the DOG-034 lexer fuses into one `F` token, read through
    `index/hook.js fTokens`. There is no second recognizer and no raw-byte scan.
 -  The indexer RESOLVES NOTHING ([BEE-002]): a ref keys on its own segments, so
    there is no ambiguity to skip and no local descent to depend on. A ref
    spelling the carrier's own path mints no row, and a binary or over-cap blob
    is never tokenised.
 -  A QUERY FANS OUT over the [BEE-001] registry: every registered index is
    opened READ-ONLY and NONE is brought up, so a stale foreign index answers
    with fewer suspects and never with a wrong one; a repo that cannot be opened
    is skipped in silence. Suspects print repo-qualified, the local repo first.
 -  FALSE SUSPECTS ARE THE PRICE of keying on text alone: no dst repo id is
    recorded, so two repos carrying a same-named file suspect each other's
    carriers, and a ref spelled deeper than the grandparent keys like a
    3-segment one. Depth and scale cost precision, never a wider probe — and a
    dst repo id would make REGISTERING a repo invalidate every other lindex mark.
 -  A rewritten history needs no special case — the mark row simply jumps, and
    an unreadable mark commit makes the next run a full tip walk.

##  The lazy contract

PRESENCE is the boundary and there is no walk ceiling (ruling 2026-08-13), so runs converge and an interrupted run keeps every commit it sealed.

 -  The tip is already this ref's MARK → no-op without even a scan; else one
    pass over the index yields the arrival state and the indexed set.
 -  ANY COMMIT IS A TIP ([BEE-005]): `bringUp(ctx, ix, {tip})` indexes from a
    commit no ref names — a diff naming one INDEXES it rather than working
    around it — and its watermark is keyed by its own COMMIT hashlet, since
    there is no ref hashlet to key it by.
 -  The walk climbs from the tip and never enters a commit already in the index,
    so a rebase needs no special case (rewritten commits are simply absent).
 -  Per commit the rev rows go in FIRST and the CPAR rows LAST: a seal persists
    a PREFIX, so a CPAR row on disk proves that commit's rev rows are on disk.
 -  A re-derived rev finds its own (path, blob, commit) triple through the `top`
    guard and writes nothing, so a re-walk never mints a second rev.

##  What is NOT indexed

The gaps, stated so a reader does not go looking for them.

 -  NO path text: `path_hl` is a one-way 40-bit hash, so the index can TEST a
    known path and can never enumerate or print one. `lindex` prints its
    suspects as TEXT the FSEG way — the index narrows, a real tree object names:
    one descent of the tip tree hashes the paths it carries and keeps the ones
    the rows asked for. No sidecar of path text is kept anywhere.
 -  NO suffix lookup in the INDEX ALONE: `dog/git/ZINF.h`, `git/ZINF.h` and
    `ZINF.h` hash to three unrelated keys; a partial path finds files only
    through the FSEG descent, which reads real tree objects for the names.
 -  NO directory LISTING: a dir carries REV-CMMT rows saying WHEN it changed
    ([LITE-044]) and nothing else — no subtree sha, no entries, no name — so
    what is IN a directory still comes from the ODB or the fs, never the index.
 -  NOT PERSISTED AT ALL: the worktree scan, the fsw rev tree and the per-wt
    status memo ([BEE-027], `index/wts.js`, `index/cache.js`, `view/wtstat.js`)
    write no row, no mtime mark and no file. They exist only in a process that
    holds a live watcher -- the pager and `bee http` -- and a one-shot CLI run
    computes once and remembers nothing: a missed event is never read as truth.
 -  NOT ASKED FOR: a bring-up walks from HEAD, or from a tip a verb NAMED
    ([BEE-005]) — never from every ref.  A commit no run has walked to has no
    CPAR rows, and `lite log` says so in plain words rather than answering
    wrong; a diff naming it brings it up instead.

